To create a calculator in JavaScript that solves the given equation, you can follow these steps:
1. Create a form in HTML that takes input values for x, y, and z.
2. Add an event listener to the form that triggers a function when the form is submitted.
3. In the function, retrieve the values of x, y, and z from the form.
4. Calculate the left-hand side of the equation using the retrieved values.
5. Check if the calculated value is equal to 18.
6. If the calculated value is equal to 18, display a message that shows the values of x, y, and z that make the equation true.
7. If the calculated value is not equal to 18, display a message that shows that the equation is not true for the given values of x, y, and z.
Here's an example code snippet that implements the above steps:
```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Calculator</title>
</head>
<body>
	<form id="calculator-form">
		<label for="carbon-footprint">Carbon Footprint:</label>
		<input type="number" id="carbon-footprint" name="carbon-footprint"><br><br>
		<label for="sustainable-practices">Sustainable Practices:</label>
		<input type="number" id="sustainable-practices" name="sustainable-practices"><br><br>
		<label for="transformations">Transformations:</label>
		<input type="number" id="transformations" name="transformations"><br><br>
		<input type="submit" value="Calculate">
	</form>
	<script>
		document.getElementById("calculator-form").addEventListener("submit", function(event) {
			event.preventDefault();
			var x = parseInt(document.getElementById("carbon-footprint").value);
			var y = parseInt(document.getElementById("sustainable-practices").value);
			var z = parseInt(document.getElementById("transformations").value);
			var result = 2*x + 4*y - 3*z;
			if (result === 18) {
				alert("The values of x, y, and z that make the equation true are: x = " + x + ", y = " + y + ", z = " + z);
			} else {
				alert("The equation is not true for the given values of x, y, and z.");
			}
		});
	</script>
</body>
</html>
```
Note that this is a simple example that assumes that the input values are always valid numbers. In a real-world scenario, you would need to add additional validation and error handling to ensure that the calculator works correctly and doesn't produce unexpected results.

