```javascript
function isPrime(num) {
  if (num < 2) {
    return false;
  }
  for (let i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
    if (num % i === 0) {
      return false;
    }
  }
  return true;
}
function rearrangeCircle(numbers) {
  const n = numbers.length;
  const visited = new Array(n).fill(false);
  const result = new Array(n).fill(0);
  function dfs(index) {
    if (index === n) {
      if (isPrime(numbers[result[0]] + numbers[result[n - 1]])) {
        return true;
      } else {
        return false;
      }
    }
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      if (!visited[i] && (index === 0 || isPrime(numbers[result[index - 1]] + numbers[i]))) {
        visited[i] = true;
        result[index] = i;
        if (dfs(index + 1)) {
          return true;
        }
        visited[i] = false;
      }
    }
    return false;
  }
  dfs(0);
  return result.map(i => numbers[i]);
}
```
This implementation uses a depth-first search (DFS) algorithm to explore all possible permutations of the given numbers. At each step of the DFS, we check if the current number can be added to the result array, based on the condition that the sum of the current number and the previous number (if any) is a prime number.
The `isPrime` function checks if a given number is prime, using a simple trial division algorithm. The `rearrangeCircle` function takes an array of numbers, and returns a new array with the numbers rearranged in such a way that the sum of any two adjacent numbers is a prime number.
Note that this implementation assumes that the input numbers are distinct, and that there exists at least one valid rearrangement of the numbers that satisfies the given condition. If these assumptions do not hold, the function may not terminate or may return incorrect results.

