To classify a given sentence as praise, criticism, advice, or request based on the context of a meeting, you can use Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques such as sentiment analysis and part-of-speech tagging. In Go, you can use the Go NLP library called "go-nlp" to perform these tasks.
Here's an example Go code that uses the "go-nlp" library to classify a given sentence as advice:
```
package main
import (
	"fmt"
	"github.com/advancedlogic/go-nlp"
	"github.com/advancedlogic/go-nlp/tokenize"
)
func main() {
	// Define the sentence to be classified
	sentence := "Please be sure to arrive on time."
	// Create a new tokenizer
	tokenizer := tokenize.NewTreebankWordTokenizer()
	// Tokenize the sentence
	tokens := tokenizer.Tokenize(sentence)
	// Create a new sentiment analyzer
	analyzer := nlp.NewSentimentAnalyzer()
	// Analyze the sentiment of the sentence
	sentiment := analyzer.Polarity(tokens)
	// Classify the sentence based on the sentiment
	if sentiment == nlp.Positive {
		fmt.Println("Praise")
	} else if sentiment == nlp.Negative {
		fmt.Println("Criticism")
	} else {
		fmt.Println("Advice or Request")
	}
}
```
In this code, we first define the sentence to be classified as "Please be sure to arrive on time." We then create a new tokenizer using the "go-nlp" library, and use it to tokenize the sentence into individual words.
Next, we create a new sentiment analyzer using the "go-nlp" library, and use it to analyze the sentiment of the sentence. The `Polarity` method of the `SentimentAnalyzer` struct returns a value of type `Polarity`, which can be either `Positive`, `Negative`, or `Neutral`.
Finally, we classify the sentence based on the sentiment returned by the sentiment analyzer. If the sentiment is `Positive`, we classify the sentence as "Praise". If the sentiment is `Negative`, we classify the sentence as "Criticism". Otherwise, we classify the sentence as "Advice or Request".

