Certainly! Here's an example code snippet in C++ where the phrase "It's too little, too late" might be applicable:
```c++
#include <iostream>
int main() {
    int size = -1;
    int* arr;
    // Prompt the user for the size of the array
    std::cout << "Enter the size of the array: ";
    std::cin >> size;
    // Allocate memory for the array
    arr = new int[size];
    // Check if the allocation was successful
    if (arr == nullptr) {
        std::cout << "Memory allocation failed. It's too little, too late." << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }
    // Populate the array with some values
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        arr[i] = i;
    }
    // Print the contents of the array
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        std::cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
    // Free the memory allocated for the array
    delete[] arr;
    return 0;
}
```
In this code snippet, the program prompts the user for the size of the array and attempts to allocate memory for the array using the `new` operator. If the allocation fails, the program prints the message "Memory allocation failed. It's too little, too late." and returns an error code. This phrase is used in this situation because the program has already attempted to allocate memory, but it's too late to do anything about it since the program has already crashed.

