To transform a sentence from simple present tense to past tense, we generally add "-ed" to the base form of the verb. However, there are some irregular verbs that have different past tense forms. 
Here's a PHP code that demonstrates the process of transforming a sentence from simple present tense to past tense:
```
<?php
function simple_present_to_past($sentence) {
  $words = explode(" ", $sentence);
  $new_sentence = "";
  foreach ($words as $word) {
    $verb = "";
    $last_letter = substr($word, -1);
    if ($last_letter == "s") {
      $verb = substr($word, 0, -1);
    } else {
      $verb = $word;
    }
    switch ($verb) {
      case "am":
        $new_sentence .= "was ";
        break;
      case "is":
        $new_sentence .= "was ";
        break;
      case "are":
        $new_sentence .= "were ";
        break;
      case "have":
        $new_sentence .= "had ";
        break;
      case "has":
        $new_sentence .= "had ";
        break;
      case "do":
        $new_sentence .= "did ";
        break;
      case "does":
        $new_sentence .= "did ";
        break;
      case "go":
        $new_sentence .= "went ";
        break;
      case "see":
        $new_sentence .= "saw ";
        break;
      case "eat":
        $new_sentence .= "ate ";
        break;
      case "drink":
        $new_sentence .= "drank ";
        break;
      case "sleep":
        $new_sentence .= "slept ";
        break;
      default:
        if (substr($verb, -2) == "ie") {
          $new_sentence .= substr($verb, 0, -2) . "ied ";
        } elseif (substr($verb, -1) == "e") {
          $new_sentence .= $verb . "d ";
        } else {
          $new_sentence .= $verb . "ed ";
        }
        break;
    }
  }
  return $new_sentence;
}
$sentence = "She drinks coffee every morning";
$past_sentence = simple_present_to_past($sentence);
echo $past_sentence; // Output: She drank coffee every morning
?>
```
In this code, we first split the sentence into an array of words using the `explode()` function. Then, we loop through each word and check if it's a verb or not. If it's a verb, we check if it's an irregular verb or a regular verb. For irregular verbs, we have a switch case statement that returns the correct past tense form of the verb. For regular verbs, we add "-ed" to the base form of the verb, unless the verb ends in "e" or "ie", in which case we add "d" or "ied", respectively. Finally, we concatenate the transformed words into a new sentence and return it.

