Regarding the impact of global warming on sea levels, the rise in temperatures caused by global warming has led to the melting of glaciers and ice caps, which in turn has caused sea levels to rise. According to the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC), the global sea level has risen by about 15 cm (6 inches) from 1901 to 2010, and the rate of sea level rise has accelerated in recent years.
The rise in sea levels has several impacts on coastal communities, including increased flooding, erosion, and damage to infrastructure. In addition, rising sea levels can cause saltwater intrusion into freshwater sources, which can impact agriculture and drinking water supplies.
To mitigate the impacts of global warming on sea levels, there are several measures being taken, including:
1. Reducing greenhouse gas emissions: This is the most effective way to mitigate global warming and its impacts on sea levels. Countries are working to reduce their carbon emissions through the Paris Agreement and other initiatives.
2. Building coastal defenses: This includes building sea walls, levees, and other structures to protect coastal communities from flooding and erosion.
3. Relocating infrastructure: In some cases, it may be necessary to relocate infrastructure such as roads, buildings, and utilities to higher ground to avoid flooding.
4. Restoring coastal ecosystems: Coastal ecosystems such as mangroves, salt marshes, and seagrass beds can help absorb the impacts of rising sea levels by reducing erosion and providing a buffer against storm surges.
Overall, it is important to take action to mitigate the impacts of global warming on sea levels to protect coastal communities and ecosystems.

