class StringComparator implements Comparator<String> {
    @Override
    public int compare(String s1, String s2) {
        if (s1.length() != s2.length()) {
            return s1.length() - s2.length();
        } else {
            int asciiSum1 = 0;
            int asciiSum2 = 0;
            for (int i = 0; i < s1.length(); i++) {
                asciiSum1 += (int) s1.charAt(i);
                asciiSum2 += (int) s2.charAt(i);
            }
            return asciiSum1 - asciiSum2;
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, the user is prompted to enter a string, and the sum of ASCII values of the characters in the string is used to set the maximum size of the priority queue. The priority queue is then initialized with this maximum size and a custom comparator that takes into account both the length and the sum of ASCII values of the strings in the queue.
The `StringComparator` class implements the `Comparator` interface and overrides the `compare()` method to compare two strings based on their length and the sum of ASCII values of their characters. The `compare()` method first compares the lengths of the two strings, and if they are different, returns the difference between the lengths. If the lengths are the same, it calculates the sum of ASCII values of the characters in each string and returns the difference between the sums.
When the priority queue is polled, the elements are returned in the order determined by the comparator, which takes into account both the length and the sum of ASCII values of the strings in the queue.

